# Home Dark visual system

## Surfaces, type, and layout

- Treat `#1a2433` as the page/popup surface and `#212c42` as the primary card
  surface. Use `#2b3850` for controls, `#f5f7fb` for primary text,
  `#91a2bb` for secondary text, `#ffb340` for the primary accent, and
  `#66758f` for muted text. Keep explicit fallbacks when theme variables are
  absent or invalid.
- Prefer Home Assistant theme variables such as `--ha-card-background`,
  `--card-background-color`, `--primary-text-color`,
  `--secondary-text-color`, `--primary-color`, `--divider-color`, and
  `--ha-card-border-radius`; card-specific `home-dark-*` variables are also
  supported by the climate card.
- Use the established rounded card language (about 20px card radius and
  compact internal spacing), strong compact typography, and responsive flex/grid
  layouts with `min-width: 0`. Do not introduce page-level horizontal overflow.
- Header and status content should remain compact, icon-led, and readable as
  one-line items where possible. Avoid unnecessary nested boxes and internal
  borders; a border is justified only when it communicates focus, separation,
  or a control boundary.
- Design for the Home Assistant Companion app first: touch targets must be
  usable on narrow screens, text must ellipsize or wrap safely, sliders need a
  generous hit area, and menus must scroll within the viewport.

## Person-card semantics

- The person card uses an exact state check: only literal `home` gets the navy
  home surface and normal theme text/icons.
- Every other state (`away`, `not_home`, a named zone, `unknown`, or
  `unavailable`) gets the gray away surface, white text/icons, and a grayscale
  avatar. Do not add a green presence border or infer a third presence tone.
- Keep away colors themeable through `--person-away-*` variables, but retain
  explicit readable fallbacks.

## Menus and popup composition

- When a themed mobile menu is required, implement a custom in-card menu/listbox
  with keyboard, focus, Escape, outside-click, and viewport-aware behavior.
  Native HTML selects can invoke platform pickers and must not be introduced for
  these menus. The existing house-status native selector is a known card-local
  compatibility exception.
- Room tiles and popup child cards are intentionally modular. Keep popup hashes,
  headings, icons, backgrounds, child-card order, and `close_on_click` in the
  Lovelace dashboard config so users can edit them without reading JavaScript.
- Preserve event isolation inside Bubble Card popups: child buttons, menus,
  steppers, sliders, and power controls must not bubble into popup open/close
  handlers.

## Implementation guardrails

- Use semantic buttons, labels, roles, `aria-*` state, and `:focus-visible`
  styles for interactive controls. Support pointer, keyboard, cancellation, and
  lost-focus paths.
- Render unavailable/unknown values as an explicit readable fallback; never
  fabricate a capability, state, entity mapping, or vendor-specific behavior.
- Keep this rule focused on stable design decisions. Verify any current dashboard
  card membership, entity mapping, resource URL, or resource ID against the
  live `home-dark` config/resource registry before documenting a change.
